---
/* =========================================================================
   ABOUT
   DRAFT — written from your 2026 resume and the About page of your old
   Weebly site. The facts are yours; the sentences are mine. Read it as a
   first draft to argue with rather than something finished.

   Three judgement calls worth checking:
     1. The Alaska passage names a friend's death. It was on your old public
        site, so I kept it — but it is the most personal thing here and the
        easiest to cut.
     2. Your partner and daughters are mentioned without names or ages. Your
        old site gave both; on a live site I would not.
     3. The framing leads with collaboration and capacity, not disaster,
        per your instruction.
   ========================================================================= */

import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/site/PageHeader.astro';
import ContactBlock from '../components/site/ContactBlock.astro';
import { cvDownload } from '../config';

/* The "Beyond the work" strip. Swap files or captions freely; images live in
   /public/images/. Alt text is required — describe what is happening. */
const personalPhotos = [
  { src: '/images/river.jpg', alt: 'Rowing a raft down a wide desert river, a child paddling a board alongside.' },
  { src: '/images/riding.jpg', alt: 'Two people in bike helmets on a green hillside on an overcast day.' },
  { src: '/images/family.jpg', alt: 'A family of five posing on the Zion National Park entrance sign in the rain.' },
  { src: '/images/paintbrush.jpg', alt: 'Wearing a headband card reading "I am a paintbrush" during a guessing game on a deck.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Background, approach, and the work of helping communities and organisations collaborate."
>
  <div class="container">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="About"
      title="Water, and the long habit of paying attention to it"
      lede="I am a coach, facilitator, and educator. Most of what I do now comes from a set of much older habits: reading conditions, working with what a place actually has, and trusting that people know things about their own lives that no assessment will surface."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="container about">
    <div class="about__body prose">
      <p>
        I grew up on the Central Coast of California, which is to say I grew up
        around water and have never really stopped organising my life around it.
        I still paddle the Sacramento River most evenings. It is not a hobby so
        much as the way I think.
      </p>

      <h2>An unlikely route here</h2>
      <p>
        My path through education was not linear. I dropped in and out of
        college, worked a run of ordinary jobs, and spent a stretch guiding
        fly-fishing clients in remote Alaska. That last one changed the
        direction of everything: a friend of mine died there, from shellfish
        poisoning that may well have been connected to a warming ocean. It made
        a set of abstract environmental arguments extremely concrete, and I have
        been working on some version of them ever since.
      </p>
      <p>
        What followed was a B.A. and an M.A. in English at Chico State, years of
        wilderness and outdoor education, a season managing a surf lodge on the
        coast of Guatemala, and eventually a Ph.D. at Utah State in the human
        dimensions of ecosystem science — a field that is mostly about how
        people actually behave around the places they live, rather than how we
        wish they would.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>
        I coach leadership teams. In practice that means helping people move
        from delivering services to a community toward building capacity with
        one — which sounds like a small distinction and is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        A needs assessment produces a plan only your organisation can deliver.
        An inventory of what a place already has produces something that keeps
        working after you leave. Most of my work is helping teams make that
        turn: finding the trusted people who hold no title, noticing which
        evidence never got collected, and building the facilitation habits that
        let a community influence a decision rather than merely comment on one.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        Through inquiry, mostly. I ask a lot of questions and I try to make it
        safe for the answers to be inconvenient. The teams I work with are
        rarely short of competence or good intent — they are short of a way to
        act together, and of permission to let what they learn actually change
        the plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        I came to this through experiential education, and it shows. I still
        think people learn leadership by doing something consequential and then
        being asked a good question about it, which is as true in a conference
        room as it was on a river.
      </p>

      <h2>Community resilience</h2>
      <p>
        For six years I managed community disaster risk reduction and adaptation
        programmes across a twenty-six county region of Northern California —
        directing a $1.2M budget, helping local partners secure more than $3M in
        external funding, and building resilience infrastructure with the people
        who would actually be using it. I now coach regional and chapter
        leadership teams across the Pacific Division on community mobilization.
      </p>

      <h2>Teaching and research</h2>
      <p>
        I have taught undergraduate composition and literature, led wilderness
        and outdoor education, and directed a U.S. State Department academic
        programme on sustainability and resilience for participants from more
        than a dozen countries. At Chico State I coordinated first-year student
        success programmes and undergraduate research, and published on
        sustainability education, belonging, mental health, and equity.
      </p>
      <p>
        My dissertation was a grounded theory study of early childhood
        experiences in nature in the Arroyo Grande Creek watershed — which is,
        I suppose, a formal way of asking why some people end up caring about a
        place and others do not.
      </p>

      <h2>Beyond the work</h2>
      <p>
        I live in Chico with my partner and our three daughters. We spend most
        of our free time outside — rivers, bikes, national parks in the rain.
        The Pacific has been the constant thread from the Central Coast to
        Alaska to now, and a fair amount of what I believe about resilience
        started as something I noticed on the water.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="about__aside">
      <img
        class="about__portrait"
        src="/images/portrait.jpg"
        alt="Nathaniel Millard, outdoors in front of a wooden fence."
        width="680"
        height="850"
      />

      <dl class="about__facts">
        <dt>Based in</dt>
        <dd>Chico, California</dd>

        <dt>Works on</dt>
        <dd>Leadership coaching, community mobilization, experiential learning</dd>

        <dt>Currently</dt>
        <dd>Community Mobilization Coach, Pacific Division, American Red Cross</dd>
      </dl>

      <p class="about__cv">
        <a class="button button--secondary" href={cvDownload} download>
          Download CV (PDF)
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="container photos">
    <h2 class="photos__heading">Elsewhere</h2>
    <ul class="photos__grid">
      {personalPhotos.map((photo) => (
        <li>
          <img src={photo.src} alt={photo.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <ContactBlock />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 16rem;
      gap: var(--space-2xl);
    }

    .about__aside { position: sticky; top: var(--space-lg); }
  }

  .about__body { max-width: var(--width-prose); }

  .about__portrait {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .about__facts {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .about__facts dt {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caps);
    color: var(--color-ink-faint);
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .about__facts dd {
    margin: var(--space-3xs) 0 0;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .about__cv { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }

  /* ---------- Photo strip ---------- */

  .photos {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .photos__heading {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caps);
    color: var(--color-ink-faint);
  }

  .photos__grid {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .photos__grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
  }

  .photos__grid img {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }
</style>
